# runtime.school — How We'll Create All 450 PySpark Practice Questions

We already have the curriculum structure: 90 lessons, 450 planned questions, and 15 datasets. Now we need a repeatable process for turning those question ideas into real, executable, automatically graded PySpark challenges.

My recommendation is to build a question-authoring system, not manually write 450 disconnected exercises or ask AI to generate all of them at once.

## 1. The complete question creation pipeline

1

Select lesson and concept

Example: L026 — Left Semi and Left Anti Joins

2

Load assigned dataset

D02 — Banking, with accounts and transactions

3

Define a unique problem

Find accounts with no posted transactions

4

Write the question specification

Business rules, input schema, output schema, edge cases

5

Create test fixtures

Visible sample plus several hidden datasets

6

Implement reference solution

Working PySpark code with verified expected results

7

Run automated validation

Test the solution, schema, nulls, duplicates, determinism

8

Publish the challenge

Editor, Run, Submit, hints, feedback, explanation

## 2. Every lesson produces five different question types

Our five-question-per-lesson rule is a useful starting allocation, but we shouldn't force every concept into the same kind of exercise.

