For performance lessons, the five slots might instead be execution-plan reading, diagnosing skew, correcting a configuration, comparing two strategies, and optimizing a reproducible benchmark.

## 3. What a finished question should look like

Here's an example of the actual experience we'd publish.

## Q0127 — Accounts Without Transactions

D02 Banking & Payments · L026 Semi & Anti Joins

Easy

A bank wants to identify accounts that have never recorded a transaction. Write a PySpark transformation that returns those accounts.

Input: accounts

| account_id | customer_id | status |
| ---------- | ----------- | ------ |
| A101       | C01         | active |
| A102       | C02         | active |
| A103       | C03         | closed |
| A104       | C04         | active |

Input: transactions

| transaction_id | account_id | amount |
| -------------- | ---------- | ------ |
| T01            | A101       | 100.00 |
| T02            | A101       | 50.00  |
| T03            | A103       | 75.00  |

Expected output

| account_id | customer_id | status |
| ---------- | ----------- | ------ |
| A102       | C02         | active |
| A104       | C04         | active |

Business rule: Include accounts of every status. An account counts as having activity if its ID appears in transactions, regardless of transaction amount.

Starter code

```
from pyspark.sql import functions as Fdef solve(accounts, transactions):    # Return a PySpark DataFrame    # containing accounts with no transactions.    pass  
```

The learner writes the transformation, clicks Run to test the visible sample, and clicks Submit for hidden tests.

The reference solution is kept out of the initial question interface:

```
def solve(accounts, transactions):    return accounts.join(        transactions.select("account_id"),        on="account_id",        how="left_anti"    )
```

The grader should compare rows as a multiset, not assume Spark produces a stable row order unless ordering is explicitly required.

## 4. How we'll create hidden tests

This is the most important part of making our questions reliable.

For Q0127, I would create the following test scenarios:

| Test | What it checks                                              |
| ---- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| T01  | Normal matching and unmatched accounts                      |
| T02  | Empty transactions DataFrame                                |
| T03  | Multiple transactions for one account                       |
| T04  | All accounts have transactions                              |
| T05  | No accounts have transactions                               |
| T06  | Null transaction account IDs                                |
| T07  | Duplicate account rows, preserving defined row multiplicity |
| T08  | Large fixture with many accounts and transactions           |

We shouldn't generate random hidden tests without knowing the expected answer. Each fixture needs a deterministic reference result.

The grading engine must validate output columns, data types, values, duplicate counts, and applicable ordering rules. It should also distinguish incorrect output from runtime errors and timeouts.

## 5. How we'll create the 450 questions efficiently

I'd build them in batches of 25–30 rather than generating everything together.

Proposed production batches

